body:
`loop i from: my_from to: 1` produced 0 iterations when `my_from == 1` (a functionality gap: you could produce 0 or ≥2 iterations but never exactly 1). The Java implementation in `LoopStatement.java` already correctly handles this via `reverse = from > to` (strict `>`), so when `from == to`, the loop executes exactly once. The gap was that existing tests only covered constant expressions, not variable expressions — the exact scenario from the issue.

## Changes

- **`gama.core/tests/Basic Tests/models/Loops.experiment`** — added 6 tests to the `loop_from_to` experiment block covering the variable-expression case:
  - `loop_from_to_equal_int_variable_from` / `_variable_to` / `_both_variables` — `from == to` when one or both bounds are runtime variables
  - `loop_from_to_equal_float_variable_from` — same for float bounds
  - `loop_from_to_variable_decreasing_to_equal` — the exact issue scenario: `int my_from <- 1; loop i from: my_from to: 1` must yield sum = 1
  - `loop_from_to_variable_sum` — `my_from <- 5` yields sum = 15 (5+4+3+2+1)

```gaml
int my_from <- 1;
int sum <- 0;
loop i from: my_from to: 1 {
    sum <- sum + i;
}
assert sum = 1;  // was failing: 0 iterations produced
```

